const index = [{"id":"design.architecture.styles","title":"Layered, hexagonal/clean, modular monolith vs microservices; REST API design","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Layers and ports organize code inside a deployable; the number of deployables is a separate choice. A module boundary that is a function call is cheaper than one that is a network call, which is why a solo developer starts with a modular monolith and a REST contract that states its concurrency rules.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.architecture.business-rules","title":"Where business rules live: app vs database","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg11","summary":"Invariants about data belong in the database, where every writer obeys them and concurrency can't slip past them; policies that need the outside world belong in the app. Rules written twice drift, and \"in the database\" is only safe if it is a constraint or holds a lock.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.architecture.reliability","title":"Reliability: timeouts everywhere, retries with idempotency, graceful degradation, observability","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Four habits keep a system quiet at night: bound every wait, make retries safe with an idempotency key the database enforces, degrade optional parts instead of failing the page, and emit one structured event per request so failures explain themselves.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.architecture.security","title":"Security across the stack: validation, SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, secrets, least privilege","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Each layer has its own attack surface and one default defense: validate shape at the boundary, keep values out of SQL text, encode at the output sink, refuse cross-site writes, keep secrets out of code and logs, and give every process the least privilege that works.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.side-by-side","title":"Paradigms side by side: Go, TS, SQL","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.23","summary":"One report written three times: Go spells out the loop, the map and the sort; TypeScript describes shapes and lets the checker find mismatches; SQL describes the result and lets the planner choose. Each puts the same rule in a different place: run time, compile time, write time.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.errors","title":"The same problem: error handling (Go errors vs TS exceptions/Result vs SQL constraints)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"One sign-up, three failure models: Go returns error values the caller inspects, TypeScript throws or returns a Result union, and PostgreSQL refuses the write with a SQLSTATE and aborts the transaction. Only the constraint still holds when two requests race.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.polymorphism","title":"The same problem: polymorphism (Go interfaces vs TS unions vs modeling variants in SQL)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.21","summary":"Discounts come in kinds: Go models them as an open set of types behind an interface, TypeScript as a closed union of tagged objects, SQL as rows with a kind column and a CHECK per shape. Each choice makes one kind of change cheap and leaves the other checked or unchecked.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.concurrency","title":"The same problem: concurrency (goroutines vs the event loop vs MVCC)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Twenty buyers, ten items: Go guards the counter with a mutex, TypeScript hides the bug behind an await on one thread, PostgreSQL fixes it with the check inside the UPDATE. Same bug, same cure in all three: make the check and the write one step.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.generics-absence","title":"The same problem: generics, and absence (zero values vs undefined/null vs NULL)","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.21","summary":"\"The cheapest offer\" can be missing because the product isn't listed, has no offers, or has an unknown price. Go answers with zero values plus an ok bool, TypeScript with T | undefined, SQL with NULL in every type and no row at all.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.paradigms.immutability","title":"The same problem: immutability","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.21","summary":"A price changes, and last month's order must keep the old one: Go protects the old list by API and copy-on-write, TypeScript by readonly types and Object.freeze, SQL by an INSERT-only table. All three make a change a new value, and all three are shallow somewhere.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.patterns.classic","title":"Strategy, adapter, decorator/middleware, observer/pub-sub — idiomatically per language","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"ts5.0","summary":"Four Gang of Four patterns in Go, TypeScript and SQL. The problems they solve are permanent; the interfaces, base classes and registries that Java needed to express them mostly disappear, and in SQL some patterns become data.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.patterns.constructors","title":"Repository, factory/constructor, builder vs functional options, DI without frameworks","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg14","summary":"Construction and wiring in Go, TypeScript and SQL: when a builder beats options, how a composition root replaces a DI container, and how PostgreSQL's search_path wires names, early or late.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"design.patterns.anti-patterns","title":"Anti-patterns: Java getters/setters in Go, deep hierarchies in TS, god packages, anemic models","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.21","summary":"Designs carried over from another language compile and run, and then fight you: Get/Set pairs and interfaces-per-struct in Go, inheritance trees in TypeScript, god packages, invariants scattered across services, and row-by-row loops against a set-based database.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-01-architecture","title":"Step 1: Architecture and request lifecycle; contract-first API with OpenAPI","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"TraceLite is one Go server, one PostgreSQL database and one React app, with openapi.yaml as the contract between them. A request goes browser, Vite proxy, ServeMux, handler, service, store, pgx, PostgreSQL and back; names change form at two well-defined places.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-02-schema","title":"Step 2: Schema: hierarchical requirements, test cases, trace links, test runs","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"TraceLite's first migration: requirements as a parent_id tree, test cases, trace links and test runs, with uuidv7 keys, named constraints the Go store can map, composite foreign keys that keep everything inside one project, and an index for every query.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-03-store","title":"Step 3: The store layer with sqlc, and transactions","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"internal/store is sqlc's generated Queries plus one hand-written Store: a WithTx that binds the same queries to a transaction, one method per use case that needs one, and the translation of SQLSTATEs and constraint names into domain errors.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-04-api","title":"Step 4: The REST API: CRUD, validation, problem-details errors, keyset pagination","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"One vertical through TraceLite's API: the handler decodes strictly and maps errors in one function, the service holds the rules every entry point shares, the store speaks SQL; failures are RFC 9457 problems, and lists page with an opaque keyset cursor.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-05-client","title":"Step 5: Typed TS client from OpenAPI + zod, wrapped in TanStack Query hooks","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.9","summary":"The client side of TraceLite's contract: types generated from openapi.yaml, zod schemas tied to them with satisfies and checked on every response, problems turned into one ApiError, and TanStack Query hooks that page with nextCursor and invalidate by project.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-06-ui","title":"Step 6: The UI: requirement tree, detail form, trace matrix, coverage indicator","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.9","summary":"The API sends the requirement tree as a flat depth-first list; the client nests it once and renders it with a component that renders itself. A detail form PATCHes only what changed, and a trace matrix toggles links optimistically while coverage stays the server's.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-07-import","title":"Step 7: File import: browser CSV upload, Go streaming validation, staging table, COPY","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"The browser sends the spreadsheet as it is, labelled with its charset. Go decodes, splits and validates it one record at a time while pgx COPYs the records into a temporary staging table; SQL checks what needs other rows, and the UI gets every problem with its line, or the whole file imported.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-08-export","title":"Step 8: File export: streaming CSV/JSON download and a traceability report","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Exports stream: pgx rows are written as they arrive, through csv.Writer or a jsontext Encoder, so memory stays flat whatever the project's size. Everything that can fail early is checked before the 200; after it, a failure aborts the connection so the download fails instead of looking complete.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-09-realtime","title":"Step 9: Real-time: Postgres LISTEN/NOTIFY, a Go hub, SSE, EventSource updating the matrix","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"A trigger NOTIFYs at commit with the project and what changed; one Go connection LISTENs and a hub fans the hint out to that project's SSE streams; EventSource in the browser invalidates the project's queries, so the matrix refetches. Every gap ends in a refetch.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-10-external","title":"Step 10: External service: standards lookup with timeouts, retries, caching; outbound webhooks","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"TraceLite calls a standards catalogue with a deadline per attempt, retries only what may go away, honors Retry-After within its own budget and serves a stale title rather than an error. It calls subscribers' webhooks the same way, signed with HMAC and carrying an event id that makes redelivery harmless.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-11-jobs","title":"Step 11: Background jobs: job table + SKIP LOCKED + a Go worker pool, with graceful shutdown","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Work that must survive restarts is a row in a jobs table: SKIP LOCKED hands each job to one worker, a lease returns a dead worker's job, run_after spaces the retries. A Go pool drains on shutdown, and failed runs queue their webhooks in the same transaction.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-12-search","title":"Step 12: Search across requirements (full-text + trigram)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"One search box, three matchers: a code prefix, Turkish words (stems or prefixes, lowered with Turkish rules on both sides) and a folded trigram key for typos and English keyboards. Results come in tiers, ranked, paged by a keyset cursor.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-13-audit","title":"Step 13: Audit history: database trigger vs application events, and the trade-offs","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"A row trigger records every change from every writer as a jsonb diff, with the user named per transaction through set_config. Application events record intent but only from code that remembers to write them. TraceLite keeps the trigger and lets the service label its changes.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-14-testing","title":"Step 14: Testing the stack: Go integration tests, Vitest + MSW, one Playwright e2e test","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Each layer gets the test that can see its bugs: Go integration tests over HTTP against real PostgreSQL for rules, constraints and the contract; Vitest with MSW for the client's handling of real responses; one Playwright test for what only a browser decides.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"fullstack.tracelite.step-15-packaging","title":"Step 15: Packaging: one binary embedding frontend + migrations; cross-compiling; customer-machine setup","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"TraceLite ships as one executable per platform: the built React app and the migrations are embedded, configuration comes from the environment, and the binary migrates, serves and shuts down in a fixed order. A customer installs PostgreSQL and one file.\n","claims":[{"tab":"Concept","text":"how step 4 puts variables into the service's environment. NSSM's and Elastic's docs use a REG_MULTI_SZ value Environment (one NAME=value per line) under HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\<name>; no Microsoft documentation of that value was found (2026-11-14), and it can't be tried here without creating a service (admin rights)."}]},{"id":"go.basics.variables","title":"var vs :=, zero values, constants and iota","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.0","summary":"Every Go variable starts at a usable zero value, := declares while = assigns, and constants are exact compile-time values that iota can enumerate.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.types-conversion","title":"Basic types and explicit conversion","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.0","summary":"Go never converts between numeric types for you, not even int to int64. Every conversion is visible, and named types like Celsius carry meaning the compiler enforces.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.strings-runes","title":"Strings, bytes and runes — UTF-8 all the way down","level":"basic","minutes":15,"since":"go1.0","summary":"A Go string is a read-only byte slice holding UTF-8. len counts bytes, range walks runes, and case conversion is locale-blind — which matters if your users write Turkish.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.control-flow","title":"if with init, the one for, switch without fallthrough, labels","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.22","summary":"Go has one loop (for), an if that can declare variables for its own scope, and a switch whose cases never fall through unless you say so.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.functions","title":"Functions: multiple returns, named results, variadic, closures","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"go1.21","summary":"Go functions return several values, and (value, error) is the idiom every API follows. Variadic parameters are slices, and closures capture variables, not copies.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.defer","title":"defer: LIFO order and when arguments are evaluated","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.22","summary":"defer schedules a call for when the function returns, in reverse order, with its arguments evaluated immediately. Deferred closures can rewrite named results; os.Exit skips them all.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.basics.pointers","title":"Pointers without arithmetic; new(expr) vs &T{}","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Go passes everything by value; a pointer is how a callee changes the caller's data. There is no pointer arithmetic, returning &local is safe, and Go 1.26's new(expr) points at any value.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.cli.fmt","title":"fmt verbs, tabwriter tables, stdout vs stderr, exit codes","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.12","summary":"fmt's verbs format any value, and widths count runes, not bytes; text/tabwriter aligns columns for you. A tool writes results to stdout, diagnostics to stderr, and reports failure through an exit code set in exactly one place.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.cli.flag-env","title":"flag and subcommands, environment variables, stdin","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"go1.13","summary":"A FlagSet per command parses an explicit argument slice and returns errors instead of exiting; environment variables feed flag defaults, so flags win. stdin is just an io.Reader.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.cli.templates","title":"text/template vs html/template","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.18","summary":"text/template and html/template share one template language and one API. html/template adds escaping that depends on where each value lands (HTML, attribute, URL, JavaScript), which is what stops XSS.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.cli.slog","title":"log/slog: levels, attributes, handlers, JSON output","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"log/slog (Go 1.21) logs a message plus typed key-value attributes; a Handler turns each record into text for people or JSON for machines. Loggers carry context with With, levels change at run time with a LevelVar.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.composite.slices-and-maps","title":"Slices and maps: len/cap, append, aliasing, nil maps, comma-ok","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"go1.23","summary":"A slice is a small header (pointer, length, capacity) over an array, so slicing shares memory and append may or may not. A map returns the zero value for missing keys, tells you with comma-ok, and iterates in random order.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.composite.slices-maps-pkg","title":"The slices and maps packages, copy, clear","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Since Go 1.21 the generic slices and maps packages, plus the min, max and clear built-ins, replace most hand-written search, sort, de-dup, filter and merge loops. Go 1.23 iterators connect them.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.composite.structs","title":"Structs, embedding, tags, value vs reference semantics","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"go1.27","summary":"A struct is a value: assignment copies its fields, shallowly. Embedding promotes another type's fields and methods without inheritance or virtual dispatch, and tags attach metadata that encoding/json and database libraries read through reflection.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.goroutines-channels","title":"Goroutines and channels: unbuffered, close, ranging","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.0","summary":"A goroutine is a concurrently running function; a channel passes values between goroutines. An unbuffered channel is a handoff: both sides wait until the value changes hands.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.select","title":"select: timeouts, default, and multiplexing","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.23","summary":"select waits on several channel operations at once and runs one that can proceed. A timer channel gives you timeouts, default gives you non-blocking sends and receives, and a for-select loop merges many inputs.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.sync","title":"sync: WaitGroup (and wg.Go), Mutex/RWMutex, Once/OnceValue, atomic","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"When goroutines share memory instead of passing it, package sync guards it: WaitGroup waits for them, Mutex and RWMutex serialise access, OnceValue runs initialisation exactly once, and sync/atomic handles lone counters and flags.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.context","title":"context: cancellation, deadlines, values used sparingly","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.21","summary":"A context.Context carries a cancellation signal, an optional deadline and a few request-scoped values down the call chain. Every function that does I/O takes it as its first parameter and stops when ctx.Done() is closed.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.patterns","title":"Patterns: worker pool, fan-out/fan-in, pipelines, semaphores, rate limiting","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Most concurrent Go programs combine a few shapes: pipelines of stages joined by channels, fan-out to workers and fan-in of their results, a bound on how many things run at once, and a limit on how often they start.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.errgroup","title":"errgroup with limits, data races, -race, goroutine leaks","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"errgroup runs a group of goroutines, cancels the rest when one fails, bounds how many run at once, and returns the first error. The race detector finds unsynchronised memory access; the goroutineleak profile (Go 1.27) finds goroutines that can never finish.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.concurrency.synctest","title":"testing/synctest: deterministic time for concurrent tests","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"go1.27","summary":"synctest.Test runs a test in a bubble with a fake clock that jumps forward whenever every goroutine in it is blocked. Timeouts, backoff and heartbeats are tested instantly and exactly, with no clock interface in the production code.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.design.packages","title":"Package design: by feature, small API surface, no util packages","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.16","summary":"A package is Go's unit of naming, privacy and dependency, and the compiler refuses import cycles. Give each package one job and a name that says it, group code by feature, and when two packages need each other, move the code or let the consumer declare an interface.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.design.constructors","title":"Constructor injection, functional options, useful zero values","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Go has no constructors, only functions named New. Make the zero value usable where you can, pass every dependency to New explicitly and wire them in main, and pick a Config struct or functional options for optional settings.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.design.layers","title":"Layered vs hexagonal: handler/service/store trade-offs","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"A handler that calls a store is a fine design until rules, a second entry point or tests without a database appear. Then a service with ports declared by the core pays for itself, and its ports must keep each invariant in one call.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.design.copying","title":"A little copying is better than a little dependency; Go proverbs, examined","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.25","summary":"The Go proverbs are design advice, not law. Some are enforced by tools, some hold almost always, and some bend: copy a five-line helper rather than import a module, but depend on maintained code for protocols and crypto, and use a mutex when a channel only adds ceremony.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.errors.errors-as-values","title":"Errors as values: wrapping with %w and adding context","level":"basic","minutes":10,"since":"go1.13","summary":"Errors are ordinary values you return and check. Wrap them with %w to add context without losing the cause, and inspect them later with errors.Is.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.errors.is-as-join","title":"errors.Is, errors.As, errors.Join; sentinels vs error types","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Sentinels answer \"which failure?\", error types carry data, and errors.Is, errors.As and errors.AsType find either anywhere in a wrapped tree. Whatever you wrap with %w becomes your API, so translate driver errors at the boundary.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.errors.panic-strategy","title":"panic/recover and an error strategy across layers","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Errors are for failures a correct program expects; panics are for bugs. Recover only at boundaries you own, the top of a goroutine and the top of a request, and let every other layer return errors upward.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.reading-files","title":"Reading files: whole file vs streaming","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.16","summary":"Use os.ReadFile when the file comfortably fits in memory; stream it with bufio.Scanner when it might not, or when one pass is all you need.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.writing","title":"Writing files: atomic writes, permissions, bufio.Writer","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.22","summary":"Buffer many small writes with bufio.Writer and always Flush; check the error from Close on files you wrote. To replace a file that matters, write a temp file in the same directory and rename it over the target.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.composing","title":"Composing readers and writers: io.Copy, TeeReader, MultiWriter, LimitReader","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.16","summary":"io.Reader and io.Writer have one method each, so small adapters wrap them like Unix pipes: MultiWriter fans out, TeeReader taps, LimitReader caps, MultiReader concatenates and io.Pipe connects a writer to a reader.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.directories","title":"Directories: ReadDir, WalkDir, path vs path/filepath, os.Root","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"os.ReadDir lists one level and filepath.WalkDir a whole tree, both in byte order. Use path for \"/\" names, path/filepath for OS paths, and os.Root (Go 1.24) to open user-supplied names without escaping a directory.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.embed","title":"embed.FS: shipping static assets in the binary","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.22","summary":"A //go:embed directive makes the compiler copy files into the binary as a string, a []byte or a read-only embed.FS. Because embed.FS is an fs.FS, templates and HTTP file servers use it directly: one file to deploy.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.csv-json","title":"CSV and JSON: encoding/csv, encoding/json (and json/v2, Go 1.27)","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"encoding/csv reads and writes RFC 4180 records; Turkish Excel adds ';', a BOM or windows-1254. encoding/json streams with Decoder and defers decoding with RawMessage. Go 1.27 ships encoding/json/v2, stricter by default.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.files-io.binary","title":"Binary data, byte order, gzip and zip","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.23","summary":"encoding/binary turns fixed-size integers and structs into bytes in an explicit byte order. gzip streams are an io.Writer and io.Reader you stack on files; zip archives need random access and untrusted entry names.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.generics.parameters","title":"Type parameters, constraints, ~, comparable, cmp.Ordered","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.21","summary":"A type parameter list says which types a function or type accepts; the constraint says what the body may do with them. Inference fills in the type arguments at most call sites, so Set[T] and Stack[T] read like ordinary code.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.generics.methods-when","title":"Generic methods (Go 1.27) and when an interface wins","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Since Go 1.27 a method can declare its own type parameters, which makes left-to-right chains like list.Filter(even).Map(strconv.Itoa) possible. Such methods never satisfy interfaces, so interfaces still own run-time polymorphism.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.handlers","title":"The handler model and ServeMux patterns","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.22","summary":"An http.Handler is one method, ServeHTTP, and everything in net/http, the router included, is built from it. Since Go 1.22 ServeMux matches methods and wildcards and picks the most specific pattern, so most services need no router library.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.json-validation","title":"JSON helpers, validation, problem-details errors (RFC 9457)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Decode request bodies strictly (size cap, unknown fields, one value), validate business rules at the edge, and answer failures as application/problem+json. Go 1.27's encoding/json/v2 makes the strict part the default.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.middleware","title":"Middleware as function wrappers: logging, recovery, request ID, timeouts","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Middleware is a function from http.Handler to http.Handler. Wrapping order is the whole design: request ID outside logging, logging outside recovery. Wrapped ResponseWriters need Unwrap, and timeouts only help handlers that watch their context.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.server-lifecycle","title":"Server timeouts, graceful shutdown, static files with embed","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"The zero http.Server has no timeouts, so one slow client can hold a connection forever: set them. Shut down with Server.Shutdown, which drains requests but cancels none, and ship static assets inside the binary with embed and FileServerFS.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.uploads-streams","title":"File upload (multipart, size limits), streaming download, SSE, WebSockets","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Request and response bodies are streams: read uploads part by part under a size cap, write downloads and Server-Sent Events as they are produced, and flush. WebSockets leave HTTP after a 101 handshake; the standard library stops there.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.http.cors-cookies-httptest","title":"CORS, cookies, and httptest","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.27","summary":"CORS is the server telling browsers which other origins may read its responses; it stops no request. Cookies carry sessions, so they need HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite and CSRF protection. httptest runs all of it in tests without a network.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.interfaces.methods","title":"Methods: value vs pointer receivers and method sets","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"go1.0","summary":"A receiver is passed like any other argument: a value receiver gets a copy, a pointer receiver shares the caller's value. Pick one kind per type, because mixed receivers let the compiler accept code that silently works on copies.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.interfaces.implicit","title":"Interfaces are satisfied implicitly","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.0","summary":"A Go type satisfies an interface just by having its methods — no implements declaration. Small interfaces plus value vs pointer receivers shape idiomatic Go APIs.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.interfaces.assertions","title":"Type assertions, type switches, any, and the nil-interface trap","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.18","summary":"An interface value is a (dynamic type, value) pair. Assertions and type switches read the type back out, and an interface holding a nil pointer is not a nil interface.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.iterators.range","title":"Range over int and range over functions (iter.Seq/Seq2)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Since Go 1.23, range accepts a function that pushes values into a yield callback. Break and return still mean what they always meant, the iterator must stop when yield returns false, and iter.Pull turns push into pull.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.iterators.writing","title":"Writing your own iterators; helper functions in slices/maps/strings","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.24","summary":"strings.Lines, strings.SplitSeq and slices.Values hand out iter.Seq values; small adapters such as Filter, Map and Take compose them into lazy pipelines that stop the producer as soon as the consumer is done.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.performance.pprof","title":"pprof: CPU and heap profiling, escape analysis (-gcflags=-m)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.24","summary":"pprof samples where a program spends CPU time and where it allocates, down to the source line; go build -gcflags=-m explains why a value lives on the heap. Profile a benchmark or a running service, fix the top entry, measure again.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.performance.allocations","title":"Reducing allocations; sync.Pool (and when not to use it); the Green Tea GC","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Every heap allocation costs once in malloc and again in GC work, and the collector runs more often the faster you allocate. Count allocations with AllocsPerRun or -benchmem, fix the hot sites with capacity and reuse, use sync.Pool only for churned buffers, and know what Green Tea changed.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.performance.cross-compile","title":"Cross-compilation and the single binary: GOOS/GOARCH, CGO_ENABLED=0, ldflags","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.24","summary":"Any Go toolchain builds for any supported OS and CPU: set GOOS and GOARCH. With CGO_ENABLED=0 the result is one self-contained file per target. Stamp versions with -ldflags -X, or since Go 1.24 let go build take them from your Git tags.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.performance.docker-vulns","title":"Multi-stage Docker builds, govulncheck, a release checklist","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Build in a golang stage, ship only the static binary in a scratch or distroless stage, and the image holds little more than your code. govulncheck reports the known vulnerabilities your code actually reaches, and -trimpath makes the binary the same wherever it is built.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.pgx","title":"database/sql vs native pgx; pgxpool sizing and lifecycle","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.27","summary":"For a PostgreSQL-only service use pgx's native API with one pgxpool per process; use database/sql where a library demands *sql.DB, and bridge the two. Size the pool from the server's connection budget, and give every connection back.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.querying","title":"Querying and scanning: CollectRows, RowToStructByName, NULL handling","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.25","summary":"pgx.CollectRows with RowToStructByName turns rows into typed structs and does the close-and-check bookkeeping for you. Give every nullable column a type that can say NULL (*string, pgtype.Text), because pgx refuses to scan NULL into a zero value.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.transactions","title":"Transactions: a WithTx helper, deferred rollback, injection-safe parameters","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Wrap writes that belong together in one helper that begins, defers the rollback and commits only on success, and run every statement through the tx it hands you. Values always travel as $1 parameters; identifiers come from an allow-list.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.bulk","title":"CopyFrom bulk loads, batches, context timeouts and cancellation","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"CopyFrom streams rows over the COPY protocol and loads 100k rows in one all-or-nothing statement; a Batch sends many statements in one round trip. Give every query a deadline, and choose whether it is enforced by closing the connection, by a cancel request, or by the server's statement_timeout.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.migrations-sqlc","title":"goose migrations and the sqlc workflow","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Schema changes are numbered SQL files applied in order and recorded in a version table; on PostgreSQL each one commits or rolls back as a whole. sqlc turns annotated queries into typed Go methods over a DBTX interface, checked against that same schema at generation time.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.postgres.repository-testing","title":"The repository pattern (and when it's overkill); testing DB code on real Postgres","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"A repository is a concrete type that owns its SQL and speaks domain values and errors; an interface appears only where a consumer needs a substitute. The rules that matter live in the database, so test repositories on a real PostgreSQL, one rolled-back transaction per test.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.client","title":"http.Client without timeouts is a bug; per-request context","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.26","summary":"http.Get and the zero http.Client wait forever. Give every client a Timeout as a backstop, give every call a context derived from the work that asked for it, and close (ideally drain) every response body so connections are reused.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.retries","title":"Retries with backoff and jitter; 429 and Retry-After; idempotency","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Retry only failures that may go away, only requests that are safe to repeat, and only inside the caller's deadline: exponential backoff with full jitter, the server's Retry-After when it sends one, and an Idempotency-Key for POSTs.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.pagination-streaming","title":"Cursor pagination and streaming large responses","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"go1.23","summary":"Wrap a paged API in an iterator that follows the cursor and fetches lazily; prefer cursors over offsets, which skip and repeat rows while others write. Stream big downloads to disk through a hash, with a stall watchdog instead of a total timeout.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.webhooks-hmac","title":"Webhooks with HMAC signature verification","level":"advanced","minutes":12,"since":"go1.24","summary":"A webhook is an unauthenticated POST until you prove otherwise. Verify an HMAC over the raw body and a timestamp with hmac.Equal, reject stale timestamps, deduplicate by event ID, and only then parse and act.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.tcp-exec","title":"TCP with net, os/exec without a shell, ReverseProxy","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.26","summary":"Below HTTP, a TCP connection is a byte stream: you choose the framing and set deadlines. os/exec runs programs directly with an argument list, never a shell. httputil.ReverseProxy with Rewrite forwards HTTP with headers you control.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.listen-notify","title":"Postgres LISTEN/NOTIFY as a lightweight message bus","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"go1.26","summary":"NOTIFY delivers a short message to every session LISTENing on a channel, at commit and only at commit. Use it as a wake-up signal next to a table that holds the real events, so a listener that was away can always catch up.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.services.grpc-brokers","title":"gRPC/protobuf and message brokers: when to choose them (concepts)","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.21","summary":"gRPC trades HTTP/JSON's readability for a generated, typed contract, binary protobuf, streaming and deadlines that travel with each call. A broker trades a direct call for time decoupling, at the price of at-least-once delivery that your consumers must make idempotent.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.testing.table-driven","title":"Table-driven tests, t.Run, t.Helper, t.Cleanup, t.Context","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"go1.24","summary":"A Go test is ordinary code: a table of cases, a loop, t.Run for named subtests, and if got != want { t.Errorf(...) }. Helpers call t.Helper and register their own t.Cleanup; t.Context ends with the test.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.testing.fakes-httptest","title":"Fakes behind small interfaces; httptest; golden files","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Test against a hand-written fake that satisfies a one-method interface the consumer declared, drive handlers through httptest, stand in for upstream APIs with a test server, and keep long expected output in golden files.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.testing.benchmarks-fuzz","title":"Benchmarks with b.Loop, fuzzing, coverage, Example functions","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.24","summary":"Benchmarks measure with for b.Loop() and run only under -bench; fuzz targets check properties on generated inputs and replay their corpus as ordinary tests; Example functions are documentation that go test verifies. Coverage says what ran, not what is right.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.testing.integration","title":"Build-tagged integration tests on a real Postgres","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"go1.25","summary":"Put database tests in files tagged //go:build integration, so go test ./... stays fast and needs nothing, and go test -tags integration -count=1 ./... runs the honest suite against a real PostgreSQL, set up once in TestMain.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.timeline.1-21-to-1-27","title":"What changed from Go 1.21 to 1.27 and how to spot old code","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.27","summary":"Seven releases changed everyday Go: per-iteration loop variables, range over ints and functions, wg.Go, os.Root, errors.AsType, new(expr), json/v2 and a standard uuid package. Knowing which version brought what tells you how old a piece of code, or a blog post, is.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.tooling.go-command","title":"The go command: mod, run, build, test, vet, fmt, doc","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"go1.16","summary":"One binary builds, runs, tests, vets, formats and documents Go code. A package is a directory of files compiled together; a module is a tree of packages under one go.mod, versioned as a unit.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.tooling.project-layout","title":"Modules, packages, internal/, and the cmd/ layout","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"go1.22","summary":"Go has two visibility levels, decided by the first letter of a name, plus internal/ directories that the go command enforces. Applications put binaries under cmd/ and everything else under internal/.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"go.tooling.modernizers","title":"go fix modernizers and the toolchain directive","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.26","summary":"go fix rewrites code to newer idioms, but only as far as the module's go line allows. The go line is both the language version and the minimum toolchain; the toolchain line and GOTOOLCHAIN decide which compiler actually runs.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.wiring","title":"How main.go wires dependencies","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"go1.24","summary":"StackLab's backend has no DI framework: server/cmd/stacklab/main.go calls constructors in order and passes each result to the next. One consumer-declared interface, httpapi.Runner, lets one if switch between running lessons in-process and in the Docker runner.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.http-layer","title":"The HTTP layer and middleware chain","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"internal/httpapi is a standard-library ServeMux with fifteen routes, wrapped in logging, panic recovery and three request guards; one problem() function writes every error, the mux's own 404 and 405 included, as RFC 9457 JSON. The run route answers 200, 400, 502 or the runner's status; a failing program is a 200.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.runner-sandbox","title":"The runner sandbox and its threat model","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"ADR-0003 promised timeouts, output caps, a process-tree kill and two run slots; internal/gorun and internal/runexec deliver them in both modes. The container limits exist only in Docker mode: in local mode (ADR-0008), lesson code runs as you, with your files, network and environment.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.sql-rollback","title":"The SQL runner's transaction-and-rollback technique","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg13","summary":"For the sql runtime, internal/sqlrun sends BEGIN, CREATE SCHEMA and SET LOCAL as one message, runs your statements one by one, and always ends with ROLLBACK, so even your CREATE TABLE never existed. A guard refuses COMMIT and ROLLBACK so your script can't end the sandbox early.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.content-pipeline","title":"Content loading and full-text indexing","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg18","summary":"Lessons go from files to the page through a build script, not a database: scripts/build-lessons.mjs validates content/ and writes an index plus one file per lesson for the frontend. There is no lessons table, no full-text index and no Ctrl+K search; this lesson shows what exists and what the planned index would take.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.ts-runner","title":"The TS CodeRunner strategy: ServerRunner vs BrowserTsRunner","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"es2022","summary":"The Run button calls runnerFor(runtime).run(request) and never asks where the code runs. The interface is one line; the real work is making a browser iframe honor the same contract as a backend process, including when a run is over.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.contract","title":"The API contract and type generation","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.18","summary":"There is no openapi.yaml and no generated code here: the run API's contract is the Go package runapi, mirrored by hand in types.ts and trusted with a cast. This lesson reads where that mirror drifted, how two of the drifts were found and fixed, and what would catch the next one.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.testing-pyramid","title":"This repo's test pyramid","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"go1.22","summary":"This repo's tests are shaped less like a pyramid than a top-heavy vase: a thin base of unit tests, a thick layer of integration tests against the real toolchains and PostgreSQL, and a lesson verifier that runs every example. Handlers, learningstore and the UI have no tests; the frontend has five small Vitest files.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"inside.codebase.compose-topology","title":"The Compose topology: who can talk to whom","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"es2022","summary":"A network boundary only protects what it separates. Reading docker-compose.yml as data shows the sandboxed runner can reach the app database; local mode, the default, has no network boundaries at all apart from loopback, database roles and the browser iframe.\n","claims":[{"tab":"Concept","text":"Docker's network docs say internal networks drop traffic to/from other networks but do not state that published ports of an internal-only container are unreachable; the claim rests on the compose comment and ADR-0008."}]},{"id":"inside.codebase.at-10x","title":"What I'd change at 10x scale","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"go1.25","summary":"\"10x\" means three different things for a single-user local app: ten times the lessons, the runs, or the learners. Each breaks something different first: serial verification (and the statically imported lesson bundle, until it was split per lesson), the two run slots, and a schema that has no user in it.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.aggregation.group-by","title":"GROUP BY, HAVING, FILTER (WHERE ...)","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"pg9.4","summary":"GROUP BY collapses rows into one row per group, and aggregates summarize each group; WHERE filters rows before grouping, HAVING filters groups after. FILTER (WHERE ...) restricts the input of one aggregate, so several conditional counts come out of a single pass.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.aggregation.agg-funcs","title":"string_agg, array_agg, jsonb_agg","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"Aggregates can build values: string_agg joins text, array_agg collects an array, jsonb_agg and jsonb_object_agg build JSON. Put ORDER BY inside the call, handle the NULLs a LEFT JOIN brings in, and aggregate each child list on its own to avoid join fan-out.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.aggregation.grouping-sets","title":"GROUPING SETS/ROLLUP/CUBE, DISTINCT ON","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"GROUPING SETS computes several GROUP BYs in one pass: ROLLUP adds subtotals along a hierarchy, CUBE every combination, and GROUPING() tells a subtotal's NULL from a NULL in the data. DISTINCT ON, a PostgreSQL extension, keeps the first row of each group in ORDER BY order.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.basics.mental-model","title":"Client/server, databases vs schemas vs roles","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"pg15","summary":"One PostgreSQL server (a \"cluster\") holds many databases; a connection is one role inside one database, and schemas are namespaces within it, resolved through search_path. Roles live above all databases, which is why they can own databases and schemas alike.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.basics.psql","title":"psql essentials: \\d, \\dt, \\x, \\timing, \\e","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"pg10","summary":"psql's backslash commands are answered by psql itself, mostly by sending catalog queries it writes for you. Learn the dozen you'll use daily, and the SQL behind \\d, which you can reuse anywhere psql isn't.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.basics.set-based","title":"SQL as a declarative, set-based language (vs loops in Go/TS)","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"pg9.0","summary":"A query describes the rows you want; the planner picks the loops, hash tables and sorts. Statements act on every qualifying row at once, against the table as it was when they started, so stop writing the loop and write the result.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.constraints.keys","title":"NOT NULL, DEFAULT, primary keys and UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"pg15","summary":"Constraints are invariants the database checks on every write, from every client. NOT NULL and DEFAULT shape columns, a primary key names the row, and UNIQUE (with NULLS NOT DISTINCT since PG 15) guards the real-world identity a surrogate key can't.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.constraints.fk-check","title":"Foreign keys and ON DELETE choices, CHECK, deferrable constraints","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.0","summary":"A foreign key makes the database refuse orphans and decide what deleting a parent does to its children. CHECK states rules about a single row, and DEFERRABLE moves a check to the end of the statement or the transaction.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.constraints.generated","title":"Generated columns (virtual by default in PG 18, vs STORED) and exclusion constraints","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg18","summary":"A generated column derives its value from the rest of the row: virtual (the PG 18 default) computes it on read, STORED on write. Exclusion constraints generalize UNIQUE to other operators, so \"no two bookings overlap\" becomes a declaration.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.files-movement.copy","title":"COPY ... FROM/TO vs \\copy","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg15","summary":"COPY moves rows in bulk between a table or query and a stream. With a file name the server opens the file on its own disk, which takes a privileged role; with STDIN/STDOUT the data rides the client connection, and that is all psql's \\copy and pgx's CopyFrom/CopyTo do.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.files-movement.csv-staging","title":"CSV import through a staging table with validation","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"Load the file as text into a staging table, compute typed values and a list of problems per row in one view, then move only the clean rows and report the rest with row numbers. COPY's ON_ERROR ignore is the quick alternative, and the example shows what it can't catch.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.files-movement.dump-generate","title":"Exporting results; pg_dump/pg_restore; generating test data with generate_series","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"pg17","summary":"generate_series plus INSERT ... SELECT seeds 100,000 realistic, reproducible rows in one statement. pg_dump exports a database as a consistent snapshot, as a SQL script for psql or an archive for pg_restore; query results leave through COPY.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.indexes.btree","title":"How B-trees work; EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE with buffers","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg18","summary":"A B-tree finds a row in a few page reads at any table size, but only EXPLAIN tells you whether the planner uses it, and only EXPLAIN ANALYZE, which really runs the statement, tells you what it cost: rows, loops and pages touched.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.indexes.plans","title":"Reading plans: seq/index/index-only/bitmap scans, nested loop/hash/merge joins","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"The planner picks one of four ways to read each table and one of three ways to join each pair, per query, from estimated row counts. Each choice tells you what it believed; EXPLAIN ANALYZE tells you whether it was right.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.indexes.composite","title":"Composite index column order, partial and expression indexes, covering (INCLUDE)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"Start from the query, not the table: equality columns first, then one range or sort column; a WHERE clause to index only the rows you search; an expression to index what you compare; INCLUDE to answer from the index alone.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.indexes.gin-brgin","title":"GIN (jsonb, arrays, full-text, trigram), BRIN for time series, skip scan (PG 18)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"GIN indexes the parts inside a value (jsonb keys, array elements, words, trigrams); BRIN stores one min/max per block range, tiny and only useful when physical order follows the column; PG 18's skip scan lets a B-tree serve queries that omit its leading column.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.indexes.stats-pagination","title":"Statistics and ANALYZE; keyset vs OFFSET pagination; the N+1 problem; pg_stat_statements","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"Keep the planner's statistics current and teach it about correlated columns; page with a keyset cursor instead of OFFSET; fetch a page's children in one statement instead of one per row; and use pg_stat_statements to find which queries actually cost the most.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.joins.inner-outer","title":"Inner, left/right/full outer, cross and self joins","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"pg7.1","summary":"A join pairs rows from two tables by a condition. INNER keeps matched pairs; LEFT, RIGHT and FULL also keep unmatched rows, padded with NULLs; CROSS pairs everything; a self join pairs a table with itself.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.joins.many-to-many","title":"Many-to-many via junction tables; semi-joins (EXISTS); anti-joins (NOT IN NULL trap)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"A many-to-many relationship is a table of links. EXISTS asks \"is there at least one?\" without repeating rows, and NOT EXISTS asks \"is there none?\" safely, while NOT IN returns nothing once its subquery yields a NULL.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.joins.lateral","title":"LATERAL joins","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"pg9.4","summary":"A LATERAL subquery or function in FROM can read columns of the items before it, so it runs per row and may return any number of rows and columns: top-N per group, expanding arrays and series, and naming intermediate expressions.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.json.jsonb-operators","title":"json vs jsonb; the operators ->, ->>, @>, ?","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg14","summary":"-> walks a jsonb document and keeps jsonb, ->> ends the walk with text; a JSON null and a missing key are different things. @> tests whether a document contains a shape, ? whether a key or string element exists. ||, -, #-, jsonb_set and subscripts change documents, with traps around NULL.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.json.path-table","title":"SQL/JSON path and JSON_TABLE (PG 17)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg17","summary":"SQL/JSON path (PG 12) walks and filters inside a document: [*], ? (@ ...), $variables, lax or strict. PG 17's JSON_TABLE turns a document into typed rows, with NESTED PATH for child arrays, and JSON_VALUE, JSON_QUERY and JSON_EXISTS extract with explicit ON EMPTY and ON ERROR behaviour.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.json.indexing-design","title":"Indexing jsonb, building JSON responses in SQL, when a real column wins","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg12","summary":"GIN serves containment and jsonpath matches; ranges and sorting on one key need a B-tree on the exact expression the query writes. json keeps the key order you build, jsonb normalizes it. A key that every row has and every query uses wants to be a real, typed, constrained column.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modeling.normalization","title":"From entities to tables: normalization 1NF to BCNF by fixing a messy spreadsheet","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg14","summary":"Normalization is \"one fact, one place\". Find the functional dependencies in the data, give each determinant a table keyed by it, and the keys and foreign keys you declare are those dependencies, enforced on every write.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modeling.denormalize","title":"When to denormalize; naming conventions, keys and audit columns","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg12","summary":"A stored copy of derivable data is a cache: decide what keeps it right before you add it. A value copied at the time of an event is not a copy at all. Conventions for names, keys and audit columns make every table predictable.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modeling.temporal","title":"History and temporal data: PG 18 WITHOUT OVERLAPS, history tables, a bitemporal introduction","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"A temporal table stores each fact with the period it was true. PG 18's WITHOUT OVERLAPS keys and PERIOD foreign keys enforce \"one at a time\" and \"only while the parent exists\"; a second period, the time the database knew it, makes past reports reproducible.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modeling.anti-patterns","title":"Anti-patterns: EAV, polymorphic foreign keys, comma-separated lists, jsonb for everything","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.6","summary":"EAV tables, polymorphic target_type/target_id pairs, lists in strings and jsonb-only tables all move the schema out of the database's sight. Each one gives up types, NOT NULL, foreign keys or statistics, and every reader pays for the checks the writer skipped.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modeling.multi-tenant","title":"Multi-tenant patterns: tenant column, schema per tenant, database per tenant, and RLS","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"Tenants can share tables (a tenant_id column), share a database (a schema each) or get a database each. With shared tables, put tenant_id into every key and foreign key, and let row-level security add the tenant filter that a query might forget.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modern.16-to-18","title":"Highlights from PG 16 to 18: uuidv7, virtual generated columns, OLD/NEW in RETURNING, temporal constraints, skip scan, async I/O, JSON_TABLE, MERGE ... RETURNING","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg18","summary":"Three releases changed a lot of standing advice: UUID keys, generated columns, audit queries, non-overlap constraints, composite index design, JSON import and MERGE. A tour with small runnable proofs, each feature tagged with its version and linked to the lesson that covers it in depth.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modifying.insert-update","title":"Multi-row INSERT, INSERT ... SELECT, UPDATE ... FROM, DELETE ... USING","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"pg10","summary":"Write sets, not rows: many rows per INSERT, rows produced by a query, and UPDATE and DELETE driven by a join. One statement, one round trip, all rows or none.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modifying.returning-upsert","title":"RETURNING, upsert with ON CONFLICT, MERGE","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"RETURNING reads back what you wrote in the same statement. ON CONFLICT makes an insert an upsert; MERGE expresses insert/update/delete in one pass with a join condition.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.modifying.soft-delete","title":"Soft-delete trade-offs","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"pg10","summary":"A deleted_at column turns DELETE into UPDATE and keeps the row. In exchange, every query, unique constraint and foreign key has to learn what \"deleted\" means, and the data is still there when the law says it must go.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.querying.select","title":"SELECT/WHERE/ORDER BY/LIMIT","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"pg13","summary":"The everyday query: name the columns, filter rows with WHERE, sort with a deterministic ORDER BY, cut with LIMIT. Clauses run in a fixed logical order, NULLs sort first under DESC, and the default sort order is not Turkish alphabetical.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.querying.null-logic","title":"NULL and three-valued logic","level":"basic","minutes":10,"since":"pg9.0","summary":"NULL means \"unknown\", not \"empty\". Any comparison with NULL yields unknown, which WHERE treats as false — the root of most NULL surprises, and the reason IS DISTINCT FROM and COALESCE exist.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.querying.patterns","title":"LIKE/ILIKE/regex, CASE, string and date functions","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"pg9.0","summary":"Compute in the database instead of in the app: case-insensitive matching with ILIKE, conditional labels with CASE, and formatted output with to_char.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.search.fts","title":"Full-text search: tsvector, tsquery, ranking, a stored tsvector with GIN","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg12","summary":"Full-text search reduces documents and queries to normalized lexemes and matches them with @@. Parse what users type with websearch_to_tsquery, order hits with ts_rank, and keep a STORED tsvector column under a GIN index.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.search.turkish","title":"The turkish, english and simple FTS configurations; Turkish text gotchas","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg12","summary":"A query must use its document's configuration: turkish stems Turkish suffixes, english and simple barely touch Turkish words, and a mismatch finds nothing without an error. to_tsvector lowercases with the database's rules, so İ/ı need lower(... COLLATE \"tr-x-icu\") on both sides.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.search.trigram","title":"pg_trgm similarity, ICU collations and case-insensitive matching, unaccent","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"pg_trgm scores strings by shared three-letter pieces: it forgives typos, not ş typed as s. An unaccent search key in an IMMUTABLE wrapper folds those away (merging ı and i), and nondeterministic ICU collations make =, LIKE and UNIQUE case-insensitive with Turkish rules.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.security-ops.roles","title":"Roles and privileges: GRANT, default privileges, a least-privilege app user","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"pg15","summary":"A role's attributes decide what it may do to the server; privileges decide what it may do to each object; ownership is a third, irrevocable power. Run the app as a role that owns nothing, and give it exactly the grants its queries need.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.security-ops.rls","title":"Row-level security for multi-tenant isolation","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg9.5","summary":"A policy turns \"WHERE tenant_id = ?\" into a property of the table: every query gets it, including the one somebody forgets to filter. Make it fail closed, force it on the owner, scope your keys by tenant, and set the tenant per transaction.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.security-ops.pooling-config","title":"Connection limits and pooling (the PgBouncer concept); shared_buffers and work_mem basics","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"Connections are processes and their number is fixed at server start; memory is one shared cache sized at start plus per-operation budgets that multiply under load. Pool connections, keep work_mem modest and raise it per transaction, and know what a pooler does to session state.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.security-ops.backup-migrations","title":"Backups and PITR (concepts); zero-downtime migrations (add, backfill, NOT VALID + VALIDATE)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg13","summary":"A dump is a snapshot; a base backup plus archived WAL can be rolled forward to any moment, which is what you need after a bad DELETE. Schema changes go out in small steps: expand, add constraints NOT VALID, build indexes concurrently, backfill in committed batches, validate, and only then contract.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.security-ops.monitoring","title":"Monitoring essentials","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg10","summary":"When the database is slow, look at what is running and waiting right now (pg_stat_activity, pg_blocking_pids, pg_locks), then at what has accumulated (pg_stat_statements, pg_stat_user_tables, pg_stat_database). Cancel or terminate the culprit, and let timeouts do that job before anyone gets paged.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.server-side.views","title":"Views and materialized views","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.4","summary":"A view is a named query that the planner expands into every query using it: no stored rows, never stale. A materialized view stores the result and is exactly as fresh as its last REFRESH; CONCURRENTLY keeps readers unblocked during the refresh, at the price of a unique index and more work.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.server-side.functions","title":"SQL functions vs PL/pgSQL; procedures","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg14","summary":"A simple LANGUAGE sql function is a named query the planner can inline; PL/pgSQL adds variables, loops and exceptions but is a black box to the planner; a procedure can COMMIT between batches. Push logic into the database when it saves round trips or must sit next to the data, not by default.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.server-side.triggers","title":"Triggers: an audit trail and updated_at; why business logic mostly belongs in the app","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg11","summary":"A trigger runs a function on every INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE, whoever issues it: ideal for mechanical invariants such as updated_at and an audit trail. The same property makes it hidden control flow, so business decisions belong in the application, where callers can see and test them.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.server-side.listen-notify-ext","title":"LISTEN/NOTIFY; extensions","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg13","summary":"NOTIFY sends a short message to every session LISTENing on a channel, only when the sending transaction commits and with no persistence; with a trigger (or the tcn extension) every committed change announces itself. Extensions add types, functions and index support per database; trusted ones need no superuser.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.subqueries-ctes.scalar","title":"Scalar and correlated subqueries; CTEs for readability; MATERIALIZED choice","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg12","summary":"A scalar subquery is an expression: it must return at most one row (none means NULL), and a correlated one is evaluated per outer row. CTEs name the steps of a query; since PG 12 a side-effect-free CTE referenced once is inlined, and MATERIALIZED / NOT MATERIALIZED override the planner's choice.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.subqueries-ctes.recursive","title":"Recursive CTEs: requirement hierarchies and trace chains, with the CYCLE clause","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg14","summary":"WITH RECURSIVE runs a start query, then repeats a step query on the rows the previous round produced until a round returns nothing: enough to walk requirement trees down and up, and trace graphs. SEARCH orders the result depth- or breadth-first, and CYCLE (PG 14) stops paths that revisit a node.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.subqueries-ctes.modifying","title":"Data-modifying CTEs","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"pg18","summary":"INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE can sit in a WITH clause, and their RETURNING rows feed the next step: moving rows, inserting a parent with its children, or updating with an audit trail becomes one atomic statement. All parts read one snapshot, so they see each other's changes only through RETURNING.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.tables-types.text-numbers","title":"text vs varchar(n), integer vs bigint, numeric for money (never float)","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"pg12","summary":"text and varchar(n) perform the same in PostgreSQL, and lengths count characters, not bytes. Use bigint wherever integer's 2.1 billion could run out, and numeric (or integer minor units) for money, because a float can't even hold 0.10 exactly.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.tables-types.time","title":"timestamptz vs timestamp, date, interval; time zones done right","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"pg18","summary":"timestamptz stores an instant and shows it in the reader's zone; timestamp stores wall-clock digits and silently drops any offset you give it. Use timestamptz for events, zone names like Europe/Istanbul instead of offsets, and AT TIME ZONE wherever a calendar day matters.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.tables-types.ids","title":"uuid with uuidv7() (PG 18); identity columns vs serial","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"pg18","summary":"Use bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY for internal keys and uuid DEFAULT uuidv7() when ids are minted outside one database or shown to users; serial is the legacy shorthand. Version 7 UUIDs sort by creation time, random version 4 UUIDs don't.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.tables-types.jsonb-arrays","title":"jsonb, arrays, enums vs lookup tables vs CHECK","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.4","summary":"jsonb suits attributes that really vary per row, arrays suit small lists read with the row; neither checks types or references. For a fixed set of values pick CHECK (simplest), an enum (ordered, compact, values never removable) or a lookup table (values that carry data or change often).\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.tables-types.alter","title":"ALTER TABLE done safely","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"pg18","summary":"Some ALTER TABLE forms only touch the catalog; others rewrite every row and index while holding a lock that blocks even SELECT. Learn which is which, and the safe order for a required column: add nullable, constrain NOT VALID, backfill in batches, VALIDATE.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.acid-mvcc","title":"ACID, savepoints, MVCC: why readers don't block writers","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"pg13","summary":"A transaction is all or nothing, and one failed statement aborts all of it unless a savepoint contains the damage. Postgres isolates transactions with row versions instead of read locks, so readers and writers never wait for each other; only two writers on the same row do.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.isolation-levels","title":"Isolation levels with live anomalies","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg1.0","summary":"Under READ COMMITTED (the default), another transaction's commit can change what your next query sees. This lesson runs two sessions side by side so you watch the anomaly happen, then prevent it.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.serializable","title":"Serializable isolation and retry loops","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg9.1","summary":"REPEATABLE READ still lets two transactions each make a correct decision and together break a rule (write skew). SERIALIZABLE detects that pattern without blocking anyone and rolls one transaction back with SQLSTATE 40001, so the application must retry it from the start.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.row-locks","title":"Row locks: FOR UPDATE, FOR NO KEY UPDATE, NOWAIT","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"pg10","summary":"When application code reads a row, decides, and writes it back, a concurrent transaction can overwrite the result. SELECT ... FOR UPDATE serializes exactly those rows; FOR NO KEY UPDATE does it without blocking foreign-key inserts, and NOWAIT or lock_timeout bound the wait.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.skip-locked","title":"A job queue with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg10","summary":"A jobs table plus SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED gives you a work queue in which workers never wait for each other. Then choose how a crashed worker's job comes back: hold the lock for the whole job, or commit the claim and use a lease.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.deadlocks-locks","title":"Deadlocks (cause and prevention via lock ordering), advisory locks","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"pg9.1","summary":"Two transactions that lock the same rows in opposite order deadlock; Postgres detects the cycle after deadlock_timeout and aborts one with 40P01. Locking in a consistent order prevents it. Advisory locks serialize work that has no row to lock.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.transactions.vacuum","title":"VACUUM, autovacuum and bloat","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg10","summary":"Every UPDATE and DELETE leaves a dead row version behind. VACUUM makes that space reusable but rarely gives it back to the OS, an open transaction can stop it from removing anything, and HOT updates with a lower fillfactor avoid much of the garbage in the first place.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.windows.over","title":"OVER and PARTITION BY: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK, LAG/LEAD","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"pg8.4","summary":"A window function computes a value for each row from a set of related rows, its partition, and keeps every row: aggregates with OVER, rankings with row_number/rank/dense_rank, neighbours with lag/lead. Windows run after WHERE and GROUP BY, so filtering on one takes a subquery.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.windows.frames","title":"Running totals, frames, moving averages, top-N per group","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"pg11","summary":"The frame is the part of the partition an aggregate or last_value reads. With ORDER BY it defaults to \"start through the current row's last peer\", which gives running totals; ROWS counts rows, RANGE measures distance on the value. Top-N per group is row_number or rank in a subquery.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"sql.windows.gaps-islands","title":"Gaps and islands","level":"advanced","minutes":13,"since":"pg8.4","summary":"Ordered rows form islands (runs of consecutive rows) separated by gaps. lead finds the gaps; for islands, subtract row_number from a fixed-step sequence, or flag each row that starts an island and number the islands with a running sum. Then GROUP BY the island number.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.architecture.folders","title":"Feature-based folders and layering: UI, state, API client","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"es2015","summary":"Group code by feature, not by file kind, and let dependencies point one way: app, then features, then shared. Features never import each other; the app composes them, and a linter keeps the rule from eroding.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.architecture.state","title":"Choosing the right home for state: server, client or URL","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"es2015","summary":"Every piece of state has one home: derived (not stored), the server cache, the URL, browser storage, or a component. Most state bugs are a value living in two homes at once, or in a home with the wrong lifetime or identity.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.architecture.patterns","title":"Idiomatic TS patterns (strategy, adapter, observer, factory) — not ported Java","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.9","summary":"The classic patterns still solve real problems in TypeScript, but functions are values and types are structural, so a strategy is a function, an adapter is a function returning an object literal, an observer is subscribe-returns-unsubscribe, and a factory needs no class.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.architecture.explicit-states","title":"Loading and error states as explicit types; functional core, imperative shell","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts2.8","summary":"Give every async value one union type (idle, loading, failed, ready, with stale data where it applies) and render it exhaustively. Put the decisions in a pure core that returns the next state plus effects as data, and keep I/O in a thin shell that only executes them.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.async.event-loop","title":"The event loop, compared with goroutines","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"es2017","summary":"A page runs your code on one thread: one task at a time, each to completion, then every queued microtask. So output order is predictable, a long loop freezes the page, and shared variables only change at await points.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.async.promises","title":"Promises and async/await, error handling","level":"basic","minutes":10,"since":"es2017","summary":"async/await is syntax over Promises: await suspends the function without blocking the thread, and try/catch around await catches the rejection.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.async.combinators","title":"Promise.all / allSettled / race / any; concurrency limits","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"es2021","summary":"The four combinators differ in one thing: what counts as done and what counts as failure. Pick by failure semantics, remember that none of them cancels anything, and bound concurrency yourself when there are many items.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.async.cancellation","title":"Cancellation with AbortController and AbortSignal.timeout; async iterators; debounce","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2018","summary":"A Promise can't be cancelled, so the platform passes an AbortSignal into the work instead, much like Go's context. Pull-based async iterators and debouncing stop or skip work at the source.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.browser-dom.dom","title":"Typed DOM queries and events; forms and validation","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"es2015","summary":"TypeScript's DOM types come from strings: tag names and event names map to precise types. Everything else is Element or EventTarget, which you narrow with instanceof, not as. Forms validate natively; you add rules and messages.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.browser-dom.reading-files","title":"Reading user-selected files: File and Blob","level":"intermediate","minutes":10,"since":"ts4.4","summary":"The browser wraps a user-selected file in a File (a Blob with a name), and hands you .text(), .arrayBuffer() and .stream() — Promises instead of the legacy FileReader callbacks.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.browser-dom.generate","title":"Generating and downloading files (Blob + object URL); drag and drop; clipboard","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2021","summary":"Build the content, wrap it in a Blob, and click an <a download> pointing at an object URL: that is a file export with no server. For Turkish Excel, write semicolons, decimal commas and a UTF-8 BOM, sorted with Intl.Collator(\"tr\").\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.browser-dom.storage","title":"localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, Web Workers, URL/URLSearchParams","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"es2022","summary":"Shareable view state goes in the URL, small preferences in localStorage, per-tab drafts in sessionStorage, and large or structured data in IndexedDB. Storage can be unavailable, so wrap it. Heavy computation goes to a Worker.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.errors.try-catch","title":"try/catch with unknown; custom error classes and cause","level":"intermediate","minutes":13,"since":"es2022","summary":"JavaScript can throw any value, so under strict TypeScript the catch variable is unknown and must be narrowed. Throw Error subclasses that carry data, wrap with cause instead of replacing, and remember that try/catch only sees rejections it awaits.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.errors.result","title":"The Result pattern vs exceptions, compared with Go; React error boundaries","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts2.0","summary":"A Result type puts expected failures in the signature, where the checker makes callers look before they use the value. Exceptions stay for bugs, and a React error boundary catches the render-time ones so one broken widget doesn't blank the page.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.everyday-types.primitives","title":"Primitives, literal types, any vs unknown vs never","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"ts4.9","summary":"Seven primitive types, one of them a single double-precision number type, plus literal types that turn values into types. any switches checking off, unknown forces a check, and never is the type with no values.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.everyday-types.objects","title":"Arrays, readonly tuples, object types, optional and readonly properties","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"es2023","summary":"Arrays, tuples and object types describe data; readonly and ? describe what may change and what may be missing. Both are compile-time only and shallow, so know when Object.freeze or a copy is the real guarantee.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.everyday-types.null","title":"null/undefined under strictNullChecks; ?. and ??","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"es2021","summary":"Under strictNullChecks, null and undefined are not members of every type: a value that may be absent says so (T | undefined, T | null), and the compiler makes you handle it. ?. and ?? are the operators that make handling it short.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.everyday-types.unions-enums","title":"Enums vs literal unions with as const; type aliases vs interfaces","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"ts5.8","summary":"Enums are the one everyday TypeScript feature that generates code, with quirks to match; a literal union derived from an `as const` array or object gives the same safety and is plain erasable JavaScript. Use interfaces for object shapes and type aliases for unions.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.functions.signatures","title":"Parameter and return types; optional, default and rest parameters","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"ts3.4","summary":"Parameters are the one place TypeScript cannot infer, so you annotate them; return types are inferred, and you pin them where they are a contract. ?, = and ... decide how a function may be called, and an options object takes over once optional arguments pile up.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.functions.higher-order","title":"Function types, callbacks, closures, this, arrow vs function, void vs undefined","level":"basic","minutes":14,"since":"ts3.4","summary":"Function types describe callbacks, and contextual typing fills in an inline callback's parameter types. Arrows capture this, methods passed as values lose it, void in a function type means \"result ignored\", and a union parameter usually beats overloads.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.generics.functions","title":"Generic functions and types, extends constraints, defaults","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts5.4","summary":"A type parameter relates the types of several values, usually an input and the output, so every call keeps its precise type. Constraints state what T must have while T keeps the rest; defaults, const type parameters and NoInfer tune inference.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.generics.keyof","title":"keyof, indexed access types, generic React components","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"es2023","summary":"keyof T is the union of T's property names and T[K] the type stored under K, so a generic K extends keyof T ties a key to its value type. The same idea makes React components generic over the caller's row type, with every column checked.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.mental-model.erasable","title":"TS = JS + erasable static types; what tsc 7 does vs what bundlers do","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"ts5.8","summary":"Types exist only for the checker: the transpiler that feeds the browser deletes them without looking. tsc 7 checks the whole program; bundlers strip one file at a time and run code with type errors in it.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.mental-model.tsconfig","title":"Essential tsconfig options and the strict flags","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"ts5.0","summary":"strict (the default since TypeScript 6.0) is a family of checks; three flags outside it close the holes that bite most often: unchecked index reads, optional properties that may hold undefined, and imports whose fate depends on elision.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.mental-model.structural","title":"Structural typing vs nominal typing; where TS runs","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"ts3.8","summary":"TypeScript compares types by their members, not their names: anything with the right shape fits, including values of unrelated classes. That follows from erasure, since at run time an object is only a bag of properties.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.narrowing.basic","title":"typeof, instanceof and in narrowing","level":"basic","minutes":13,"since":"ts5.5","summary":"TypeScript follows your runtime checks (typeof, instanceof, in, equality and truthiness) and narrows a union branch by branch. Each check tests exactly what JS tests: typeof null is \"object\", truthiness also drops 0 and \"\", and instanceof needs a class.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.narrowing.discriminated-unions","title":"Discriminated unions: the single most important pattern","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"ts2.0","summary":"Give every variant of a union a shared literal tag field. The compiler then narrows the variant from the tag alone, and a switch over the tag can be checked for exhaustiveness with never.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.narrowing.guards","title":"Type guards, assertion functions, satisfies, never for exhaustiveness","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.9","summary":"Beyond discriminated unions: custom type predicates teach the checker your own invariants, assertion functions throw instead of branching, satisfies validates without widening, and never catches a missed case at compile time.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.node.type-stripping","title":"Node running .ts via type stripping; erasable syntax only","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"ts5.8","summary":"Node 22.18+ and 23.6+ run a .ts file directly by blanking out its type syntax with spaces: no build step, no tsconfig, no type check. Only erasable syntax survives that, which is exactly what erasableSyntaxOnly makes tsc enforce.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.node.fs-streams","title":"node:fs/promises and streams, side by side with Go equivalents","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2018","summary":"The Go file-reading lesson again, in Node: readFile for the whole file, FileHandle.readLines() for constant memory, and stream.pipeline as the io.Copy that propagates errors and closes every stage. Chunks are bytes, so decode them with the stream, not one by one.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.node.cli-server","title":"A small CLI and a tiny HTTP server in Node, next to the Go version","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"es2024","summary":"The same two programs as in the Go track: a CLI built on util.parseArgs and stdin, and an HTTP server built on node:http and fetch. The code looks alike; the runtime differs. Every Node request shares one thread, so CPU work in a handler stalls them all, and a throw in an async handler can kill the process.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.objects-classes.spread","title":"Spread/rest and immutability","level":"basic","minutes":12,"since":"es2023","summary":"Spread copies one level, so a functional update copies every object on the path to the change and shares the rest. structuredClone deep-copies data; no copy, deep or shallow, carries a class's behaviour.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.objects-classes.classes","title":"Classes: #private vs private, readonly, abstract, parameter properties","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2022","summary":"`#private` is enforced by the JavaScript engine; `private`, `protected`, `readonly` and `abstract` are compile-time checks that vanish. Parameter properties generate code and interact badly with field initializers, so erasable code declares fields explicitly.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.objects-classes.modules","title":"ES modules: import type, verbatimModuleSyntax, barrels, path aliases, .d.ts","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"ts5.0","summary":"ES modules run once, share one instance per specifier and export live, read-only bindings. Mark type-only imports with `type` so every tool emits the same code, import from the defining file instead of a barrel, and configure path aliases for both tsc and the runtime.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.components","title":"Typed components and props: children, ComponentProps","level":"intermediate","minutes":12,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"A component is a function of one typed props object, and children is just another prop. Extend native elements with ComponentPropsWithoutRef and derive prop types from the types you already have instead of copying fields.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.state","title":"useState and useReducer with discriminated unions","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"State is a snapshot per render that you replace, never mutate. Model it as one discriminated union instead of independent flags, and move the transitions into a reducer when they need rules: impossible states then fail to compile.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.effects-hooks","title":"Effects and cleanup (and when you don't need an effect); custom hooks; context","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"An effect keeps a component synchronized with something outside React, and its cleanup undoes exactly what its setup did. Values computed from props belong in render, not in an effect. Custom hooks share logic; context injects values down the tree.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.forms-lists","title":"Controlled forms, lists and keys, accessibility basics","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"A controlled input renders React state and reports every keystroke through onChange; the form validates on submit. useId wires labels and error messages for assistive technology, and list keys come from the data, never from the index.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.query","title":"TanStack Query: caching, invalidation, optimistic updates","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"Server data is a cache, not component state. useQuery reads it by key with deduplication and stale-while-revalidate refetching; useMutation changes the server, updates the cache optimistically, rolls back on error and invalidates what it touched.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.react.routing","title":"Routing and URL state; the rendering model and memoization; composition","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"Put navigation state (which page, which filter) in the URL so back, reload and shared links work. A component re-renders when its parent does; memo, useCallback and passing children skip that work, and the first two are worth it only for a problem you have measured.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.services.fetch","title":"fetch basics and pitfalls: it doesn't throw on 4xx/5xx","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2022","summary":"fetch rejects only when no HTTP response arrives; a 404 or a 500 fulfils the promise like a 200. Check response.ok, read the body exactly once, and pass a timeout signal, because fetch has no timeout of its own.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.services.zod","title":"Runtime validation with zod at the trust boundary","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"es2022","summary":"TypeScript types are erased, so nothing checks what arrives over the network. A zod schema is a run-time value that validates and normalizes the data, and the static type is derived from it: one definition, checked where data enters the app.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.services.openapi","title":"Generating types from OpenAPI; a typed API client","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"es2022","summary":"An OpenAPI document is the contract between server and client. openapi-typescript turns it into types with no run-time code, and a small generic client checks paths, parameters and per-status bodies at compile time. Nothing checks the server at run time, so drift needs a validator or a test.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.services.advanced","title":"Retries with backoff, timeouts, pagination, uploads with progress","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"es2022","summary":"A production client retries only what is safe to repeat, with exponential backoff, jitter and the server's Retry-After; pages with server-issued cursors instead of offsets; and reports progress by counting streamed bytes, or by uploading in parts, because fetch has no upload progress events.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.services.realtime-cors","title":"WebSockets, EventSource (SSE), CORS from both sides, tokens vs cookies (concepts)","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"es2022","summary":"Server-sent events push text over one HTTP response; WebSockets carry messages both ways. CORS doesn't stop requests: the server labels its response and the browser decides whether the page's origin, even the opaque \"null\", may read it.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.testing.vitest","title":"Vitest: it.each tables, Testing Library (behavior, not implementation)","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"Vitest runs tests the way Vite runs code, and it.each turns a table of cases into one named test per row, the TS spelling of Go's table-driven tests. Testing Library drives components through roles, labels and clicks, so the tests survive a rewrite of the component's internals.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.testing.msw-playwright","title":"MSW for the API; Playwright basics; type tests with expectTypeOf","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"Mock the network, not fetch: MSW answers real requests from production code. Playwright drives a real browser and can answer its requests too, so end-to-end tests need no backend. expectTypeOf tests the types themselves, which no runtime test can see.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.tooling.lint","title":"A ladder of tsconfig strictness; ESLint + typescript-eslint; Prettier","level":"intermediate","minutes":14,"since":"ts6.0","summary":"Three tools, three jobs: tsc proves types, type-aware ESLint catches bugs that type-check fine, Prettier ends formatting debates. Climb the strictness ladder one measured rung at a time, and ratchet each new check in so existing errors never block a merge.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.tooling.vite","title":"Vite: dev server, proxy, env variables, build, source maps, DevTools debugging","level":"intermediate","minutes":15,"since":"es2020","summary":"Vite serves source files to the browser as native ES modules in development and bundles them for production. Neither mode type-checks. Its proxy keeps the API same-origin, its env variables are build-time strings, and source maps take DevTools back to your TypeScript.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.tooling.bundle","title":"Bundle-size awareness; the TS 7 native compiler and what changed since 5/6","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts7.0","summary":"Every kilobyte in the first chunk is downloaded, parsed and run before the page works: measure it, delete what the platform already provides, and split the rest with import(). TypeScript 7 is the same type checker as 6.0, rewritten in Go: far faster, with 6.0's deprecations removed and no compiler API until 7.1.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.type-level.utility","title":"Mapped types and the built-in utilities: Partial, Pick, Omit, Record, Awaited...","level":"advanced","minutes":14,"since":"ts4.5","summary":"Write the domain type once and compute its variants: the create payload, the patch, the per-field error map, the result of an async function. Mapped types are the loop behind every built-in utility, and you can write your own in one line.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.type-level.conditionals","title":"Conditional types, infer, template literal types","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts4.1","summary":"A conditional type is an if-expression over types, distributed over unions; infer extracts parts of what matched, including parts of strings. They compute precise types for callers, but generic code that must produce them needs casts, which is where to stop.\n","claims":[]},{"id":"ts.type-level.branded","title":"Branded types (RequirementId) and making illegal states unrepresentable","level":"advanced","minutes":15,"since":"ts3.5","summary":"A brand gives a structural type a name the checker enforces, so a RequirementId can only come from the parser that validated it. Unions model each state with its own data. Both pay off where values get mixed up or states get confused, and stop paying beyond that.\n","claims":[]}];
export default index;
